<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gordian II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 192–238</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gordian II was Roman emperor for about three weeks in 238, jointly with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a>. When a revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-djem">Thysdrus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> proclaimed the elderly proconsul emperor against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a>, the Senate recognised both. Gordian II was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> fighting the Numidian legion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capelianus">Capelianus</a>, and his father hanged himself; his nephew later reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii">Gordian III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gordian-ii.jpg" alt="Gordian II" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gordiano_II_-_ritratto_da_sesterzio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gordian II, in Latin Marcus Antonius Gordianus Sempronianus Romanus, was the only known son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a>, who was said to be related to prominent senators. His first two names suggest that his paternal ancestors received Roman citizenship under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> or one of his daughters, and his cognomen Gordianus suggests family origins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, especially Galatia and Cappadocia. His younger sister Maecia Faustina was the mother of the later emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii">Gordian III</a>.</p><p>The only surviving account of his early career is the Historia Augusta, a source that cannot be taken as reliable. It has him serve as quaestor under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> and as praetor and consul suffect under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a>, and it names his mother as Fabia Orestilla, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, a claim modern historians dismiss. Some evidence suggests that his mother may instead have been a granddaughter of the Greek sophist Herodes Atticus. In 237 or 238 he went to the province of Africa Proconsularis as legate under his father, the proconsular governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in 235 mutinous troops at Moguntiacum, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, killed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a> and his mother Julia Avita Mamaea, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a> became emperor despite the disapproval of the Senate. In 238, probably at the end of March, a local elite that had just killed Maximinus's procurator forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a> into a full-scale revolt. Because of his father's age, the younger Gordian, said to be 46, was joined to the throne and acclaimed augustus, and like his father received the cognomen Africanus. Because Maximinus was unpopular, the Senate and most of the other provinces ratified their claim.</p><p>Opposition came from the neighbouring province of Numidia. Its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capelianus">Capelianus</a>, a loyal supporter of Maximinus who held a grudge against Gordian, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> with the legion III Augusta, the only legion stationed in the region, and other veteran units. Gordian II led a militia of untrained soldiers, lost the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and was killed. According to the Historia Augusta his body was never recovered. On hearing the news his father killed himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gordian II died in April 238, and because he died before his father his reign of about 22 days is the shortest recorded of any Roman emperor. The rebellion failed, but by the end of 238 his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii">Gordian III</a> was recognised as emperor by the whole Roman world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a>, in The History of the Decline and Fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, wrote that twenty-two acknowledged concubines and a library of sixty-two thousand volumes attested to the variety of his inclinations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gordian II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 238 the legion's loyalty decided a civil war: when a revolt at Thysdrus proclaimed the elderly proconsul Gordian I emperor against Maximinus Thrax, the legate of Numidia, Capelianus, marched on Carthage, killed <b>Gordian II</b> in battle and drove his father to suicide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Roman Carthage. Died in Roman Carthage. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
